
1. Why Spring Boot? Pros and Cons
Spring Boot is a framework built on top of Spring that simplifies application development by providing auto-configuration, embedded servers, and production-ready features.
Some advantages are faster development, reduced configuration, easier dependency management through starters, and built-in monitoring with Actuator.
The disadvantages are that it may consume more memory compared to lightweight frameworks, auto-configuration can sometimes hide implementation details, and startup time can be longer for large applications.
Overall, Spring Boot is a great choice for building enterprise-level REST APIs and microservices quickly.

2. How do you start a Spring Boot project from scratch?
When starting a Spring Boot project, I usually go to Spring Initializr and select the required dependencies such as Spring Web, Spring Data JPA, Spring Security, Lombok, and MySQL Driver.
After generating the project, I configure the application.properties file, create the package structure including controller, service, repository, and entity layers, then implement business logic and database integration.
Finally, I test the APIs using Postman and package the application using Maven or Gradle.

3. @Controller vs @RestController
@Controller is mainly used for traditional MVC applications that return views such as JSP or Thymeleaf pages.
@RestController is a combination of @Controller and @ResponseBody. It automatically converts returned objects into JSON or XML and sends them directly to the client.
For modern RESTful APIs, I usually use @RestController because most frontend applications consume JSON responses.

4. @PathVariable vs @RequestParam
@PathVariable is used to extract values from the URL path itself
For example, '/users/100' can map 100 to a path variable.
@RequestParam is used to read query parameters from the URL, such as '/users?id=100'.
I use @PathVariable when identifying a specific resource and @RequestParam when filtering, sorting, or searching data.

5. @RequestBody vs @ResponseBody
@RequestBody is used to convert incoming JSON data into a Java object.
For example, when creating a new user, the JSON request body is automatically mapped to a User object.
@ResponseBody tells Spring to serialize the returned Java object into JSON and send it back to the client.
In practice, @RestController already includes @ResponseBody automatically.

6. How do you use GetMapping, PostMapping, PutMapping, DeleteMapping, and RequestMapping?
These annotations map HTTP requests to controller methods.
@GetMapping is used for retrieving data.
@PostMapping is used for creating resources.
@PutMapping is used for updating resources.
@DeleteMapping is used for deleting resources.
@RequestMapping is more general and can handle multiple HTTP methods.
Using these annotations helps follow RESTful API design principles and makes the code easier to understand.

7. What is Spring Actuator?
Spring Actuator provides production-ready monitoring and management features for Spring Boot applications.
It exposes endpoints such as health, metrics, beans, environment information, and application status.
For example, the '/actuator/health' endpoint can be used by monitoring systems to verify whether the application is running properly.
It is very useful for observability and system monitoring in production environments.

8. How do you achieve async in a Spring Boot application?
Spring Boot supports asynchronous execution through the @Async annotation.
First, I enable asynchronous processing using @EnableAsync.
Then I annotate a service method with @Async.
When the method is called, Spring executes it in a separate thread from the thread pool.
This is useful for tasks like sending emails, generating reports, or calling external services without blocking the main request thread.

