10. How does Spring validate data?
Spring commonly uses Bean Validation with Jakarta Validation annotations.
For example, I can use @NotNull, @NotBlank, @Email, and @Size on entity or DTO fields.
Then I add @Valid to the controller method parameter.
If validation fails, Spring automatically throws an exception, which can be handled by a global exception handler to return meaningful error messages.

11. How does Spring do logging?
Spring Boot uses SLF4J as the logging abstraction and Logback as the default logging implementation.
I typically create a logger using LoggerFactory and use different log levels such as DEBUG, INFO, WARN, and ERROR.
In production, I usually log important business events, exceptions, and system status while avoiding sensitive information.
Proper logging helps with troubleshooting and monitoring.

12. Cache Hit vs Cache Miss
A cache hit occurs when the requested data is found in the cache, so the application can return it immediately.
A cache miss occurs when the data is not found in the cache, and the application must retrieve it from the database or another source.
Cache hits improve performance and reduce database load, while cache misses are slower because additional processing is required.

13. Do some research on Redis and have a basic understanding
Redis is an in-memory key-value data store commonly used for caching, session management, distributed locking, and message queues.
Because data is stored in memory, Redis is much faster than traditional databases.
It supports various data structures such as strings, lists, sets, hashes, and sorted sets.
In Spring Boot, Redis is often used together with Spring Cache to improve application performance and reduce database access.

14. Why use @RestControllerAdvice instead of @ControllerAdvice?
@ControllerAdvice provides global exception handling and shared controller functionality.
However, when building REST APIs, I usually use @RestControllerAdvice because it combines @ControllerAdvice and @ResponseBody.
This means exception responses are automatically converted to JSON without requiring additional annotations.
Since most modern applications expose REST APIs, @RestControllerAdvice is more convenient and reduces boilerplate code.


